WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:03.837
In 1971

00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:06.423
National Airlines released 
this advertisement.

00:00:06.548 --> 00:00:10.176
It pictured a real flight attendant 
and a new slogan:

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:11.177
Fly me.

00:00:11.177 --> 00:00:13.304
“All over Florida, fly me.”

00:00:13.304 --> 00:00:17.559
Within months, the campaign included 
Jo, Denise, and Laura.

00:00:17.684 --> 00:00:20.520
The airlines basically tried to sell 
the stewardesses

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.355
as sex objects.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:23.440
“I'm Dianne.”

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:24.190
“Fly me.”

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.151
“I’m Terry. 
Fly me.”

00:00:26.151 --> 00:00:27.861
“I'm Maurica. 
Fly me.”

00:00:27.861 --> 00:00:28.862
And it worked.

00:00:28.862 --> 00:00:30.405
After the Fly Me campaign

00:00:30.405 --> 00:00:33.450
National saw a 19% jump 
in ticket sales.

00:00:33.783 --> 00:00:35.827
Soon, other airlines followed.

00:00:36.036 --> 00:00:38.955
This was one of the many ways 
that the airline industry

00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:40.415
degraded flight attendants

00:00:40.415 --> 00:00:43.209
and discriminated against them 
for decades....

00:00:43.585 --> 00:00:46.796
until flight attendants got together 
and pushed back.

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:49.632
It was pretty spectacular 
to be in a room

00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:51.009
full of other flight attendants

00:00:51.009 --> 00:00:52.594
who were saying enough is enough.

00:00:52.594 --> 00:00:55.430
And I was just not going to sit back
and allow this to continue.

00:00:55.472 --> 00:00:57.932
Flight attendants changed 
the airline industry

00:00:57.932 --> 00:01:00.852
and became one of the first groups 
in the US

00:01:00.852 --> 00:01:03.104
to fight discrimination in the workplace.

00:01:03.146 --> 00:01:04.731
They truly paved the way

00:01:04.731 --> 00:01:06.649
for working women 
in the United States today

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:08.610
and nobody knows about them.

00:01:14.991 --> 00:01:16.785
“This working jet set

00:01:16.785 --> 00:01:19.120
who breakfast in New York 
and lunch in Phoenix.”

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:22.874
Throughout the 50s and 60s, 
becoming a flight attendant

00:01:22.874 --> 00:01:24.626
known as a stewardess 
at the time

00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:27.128
was one of the most coveted jobs 
for women.

00:01:27.295 --> 00:01:29.964
If you were a young woman and 
your other employment opportunities

00:01:29.964 --> 00:01:32.592
were things like teacher or nurse

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:34.719
or maybe a man’s secretary

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:37.388
the idea you could be jet setting 
around the country

00:01:37.388 --> 00:01:39.182
talking to men like Don Draper...

00:01:39.182 --> 00:01:40.600
“Where are you saying?”

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:41.434
“Belvedere.”

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.895
You can see how appealing 
that might have been.

00:01:43.895 --> 00:01:47.315
The airlines sold an elite, 
glamorous image of air travel...

00:01:47.857 --> 00:01:51.277
and relied on stewardesses 
to deliver that message.

00:01:51.277 --> 00:01:54.405
“The smiling hostess or stewardess 
who anticipates

00:01:54.405 --> 00:01:56.366
the needs of every passenger.”

00:01:57.033 --> 00:01:58.409
To make sure stewardesses

00:01:58.409 --> 00:02:01.538
match the standards that were 
advertised to passengers.

00:02:01.538 --> 00:02:05.375
Airlines came up with incredibly strict 
hiring practices.

00:02:05.375 --> 00:02:06.960
“To qualify on most airlines...

00:02:07.085 --> 00:02:11.005
She must be healthy, single, and 
between the ages of 21 and 28.”

00:02:11.214 --> 00:02:15.844
“She must be between 5’2” and 5’6”
in height and of normal weight.”

00:02:15.844 --> 00:02:19.848
You had to have straight teeth, 
clear skin, no glasses.

00:02:20.014 --> 00:02:22.100
You had to be extremely slim.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.560
When I first applied

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:26.479
had a male interview at that time 
who measured my hips.

00:02:26.729 --> 00:02:29.774
Paula Mariedaughter flew over 3 million miles

00:02:29.774 --> 00:02:32.443
as a stewardess and model for TWA.

00:02:32.610 --> 00:02:35.613
And it was pretty much 
the standard fare

00:02:35.613 --> 00:02:37.866
in terms of how they treated 
young women.

00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:41.703
The process was designed to make 
the job feel exclusive.

00:02:42.162 --> 00:02:45.456
One ad for Eastern Airlines 
even presented the losers...

00:02:45.665 --> 00:02:48.626
showcasing that they pass up 
around 19 girls

00:02:48.626 --> 00:02:50.962
before getting one that qualifies.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:53.548
“They’re probably good enough to get 
a job anywhere they want.”

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:55.383
“But at Eastern, we're very choosy

00:02:55.383 --> 00:02:57.427
about whom we let serve you on a plane.”

00:02:57.427 --> 00:02:58.178
“She's awkward.”

00:02:59.512 --> 00:03:00.972
“She wears glasses.”

00:03:00.972 --> 00:03:02.599
But the biggest one was...

00:03:02.599 --> 00:03:05.143
“Oh! N-aww. 
She's married.”

00:03:05.602 --> 00:03:07.061
You couldn't be married.

00:03:07.061 --> 00:03:09.272
A male business traveler 
could get on the airplane...

00:03:09.606 --> 00:03:12.901
look around and know that 
all of these stewardesses

00:03:12.901 --> 00:03:15.278
were theoretically available.

00:03:15.278 --> 00:03:17.864
That's where age limits 
played a big role, too.

00:03:17.989 --> 00:03:20.116
Airlines wanted to keep 
the workforce young

00:03:20.116 --> 00:03:23.536
and some forced women to retire 
as young as 27.

00:03:23.745 --> 00:03:26.372
A lot of these hiring restrictions 
were explicit

00:03:26.372 --> 00:03:27.790
but there was one standard

00:03:27.790 --> 00:03:30.793
that was unwritten, 
yet consistently enforced.

00:03:31.419 --> 00:03:33.046
You had to be white.

00:03:33.046 --> 00:03:35.256
They did not want us on board.

00:03:35.256 --> 00:03:37.717
The fight to take down 
these industry standards

00:03:37.717 --> 00:03:40.386
started with black women 
like Patricia Banks.

00:03:40.428 --> 00:03:43.723
My name was Patricia Noisette Banks Edmiston...

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.309
I became the first black flight attendant

00:03:46.309 --> 00:03:48.436
on a commercial aircraft in 1960.

00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:49.896
I saw an advertisement

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:52.273
for the Grace Downs Air Career School
and I said

00:03:52.273 --> 00:03:53.858
oh wow, this sounds great.

00:03:53.858 --> 00:03:55.193
I think I'd like to do this.

00:03:55.193 --> 00:03:58.321
And I always thought it would be interesting
to travel around the world

00:03:58.321 --> 00:04:00.114
and see what other places were like.

00:04:00.114 --> 00:04:02.033
And I was accepted into the school.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:04.327
Patricia was the only black student

00:04:04.327 --> 00:04:06.287
at Grace Downs Air Career School.

00:04:06.287 --> 00:04:09.123
After completing the program in 1956

00:04:09.123 --> 00:04:12.293
she interviewed with TWA, 
Mohawk, and Capital

00:04:12.293 --> 00:04:15.630
which would later merge with United
for a stewardess position.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:18.007
But Patricia never heard back.

00:04:18.216 --> 00:04:20.385
Other people were getting responses

00:04:20.385 --> 00:04:22.595
and I wasn't getting any response whatsoever.

00:04:22.637 --> 00:04:24.597
It was painful because I kept wondering:

00:04:24.597 --> 00:04:25.890
Well, what's wrong here?

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.642
And then a chief stewardess

00:04:27.642 --> 00:04:30.561
saw me outside of the school 
and she looked at me...

00:04:30.853 --> 00:04:33.189
and she said, “Pat, I hate to see you
go through this...

00:04:33.398 --> 00:04:35.984
but the airlines do not hire Negroes.”

00:04:36.192 --> 00:04:37.610
Patricia filed a case 

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:40.738
with the New York State Commission 
Against Discrimination 

00:04:40.738 --> 00:04:44.701
to investigate Capital airlines 
for racist hiring practices.

00:04:44.701 --> 00:04:46.494
During the process

00:04:46.494 --> 00:04:49.914
I had a lot of threats of 
being raped, murdered.

00:04:50.123 --> 00:04:53.626
I had the police involved at certain times
during the beginning.

00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.129
It was not easy, but I just feel

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:58.756
it was something that I had to do.

00:04:58.756 --> 00:05:00.383
I had to do.

00:05:00.383 --> 00:05:02.176
The commission decided that the airline

00:05:02.176 --> 00:05:05.888
had discriminated against Patricia 
by maintaining a policy

00:05:05.888 --> 00:05:08.308
that barred black applicants 
from employment.

00:05:08.349 --> 00:05:09.600
In 1960

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.521
Capital was ordered to reverse 
that policy and hire Patricia.

00:05:13.563 --> 00:05:16.274
The respondent Capital Airlines Inc.

00:05:16.274 --> 00:05:21.696
shall cease and desist from refusing to hire 
or employ complainant

00:05:21.696 --> 00:05:25.241
Patricia Banks as a flight hostess 
because of her color

00:05:25.241 --> 00:05:28.953
and maintaining a policy of barring Negroes

00:05:28.953 --> 00:05:30.955
from employment because of their color

00:05:30.955 --> 00:05:32.540
in all flight capacities

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:34.959
including that of flight hostess.

00:05:35.752 --> 00:05:37.003
By winning her case

00:05:37.003 --> 00:05:40.506
she became one of the first black
commercial flight attendants.

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:43.176
I wanted to work hard to break this barrier.

00:05:43.176 --> 00:05:45.428
It was something I was doing for my people

00:05:45.428 --> 00:05:47.972
because I didn't care whether 
I got hired or not.

00:05:47.972 --> 00:05:50.558
But some young black woman 
was going to fly.

00:05:50.725 --> 00:05:53.227
Patricia's victory was the beginning.

00:05:53.227 --> 00:05:55.772
“Congress passes the most sweeping civil rights bill

00:05:55.772 --> 00:05:57.315
ever to be written into the law.”

00:05:57.315 --> 00:06:00.818
A few years later, when the Civil Rights Act 
became federal law

00:06:00.860 --> 00:06:04.113
employment discrimination 
on the basis of race and sex

00:06:04.113 --> 00:06:07.158
was finally prohibited under Title VII of the act.

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:09.702
More black women began to challenge airlines

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:12.497
for the racism they experienced in the industry...

00:06:12.663 --> 00:06:15.458
and one by one, they secured their right to fly.

00:06:15.541 --> 00:06:18.836
By 1965, there were 50 black stewardesses

00:06:18.836 --> 00:06:22.298
working at 7 of the largest US carriers.

00:06:22.298 --> 00:06:26.302
Title VII was a turning point 
for stewardesses all around.

00:06:26.302 --> 00:06:29.806
The same legislation that put an end 
to racist practices...

00:06:30.014 --> 00:06:31.724
became a tool for stewardesses

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:34.394
to put an end to sexist policies at work.

00:06:34.602 --> 00:06:37.146
Title seven was mostly put in place to try and fight

00:06:37.146 --> 00:06:38.606
racism in employment

00:06:38.606 --> 00:06:39.899
but the flight attendants were

00:06:39.899 --> 00:06:42.819
the first people to see that like, 
Oh, they could use this

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:45.196
to fight sex discrimination in employment.

00:06:45.405 --> 00:06:49.492
Their persistent legal action
kicked off a stewardess rebellion.

00:06:49.826 --> 00:06:52.912
In the first 18 months of Title VII becoming law

00:06:52.912 --> 00:06:57.250
stewardesses lodged more than 
100 employment discrimination cases 

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:01.796
and used those rulings to challenge airlines 
directly with the help of their union.

00:07:01.838 --> 00:07:04.966
One of those challenges was launched in 1965

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:06.426
by Betty Green Bateman

00:07:06.426 --> 00:07:08.928
who was fired after Braniff Airlines discovered

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:11.722
she had been secretly married 
for more than a year.

00:07:12.223 --> 00:07:14.475
After months of fighting with the airline...

00:07:14.684 --> 00:07:16.936
she was finally allowed to keep her job.

00:07:17.019 --> 00:07:20.523
The Bateman case was the start 
of the sort of dominoes falling

00:07:20.523 --> 00:07:21.774
with the marriage rules.

00:07:21.774 --> 00:07:23.818
The lawsuit forced multiple airlines

00:07:23.818 --> 00:07:25.653
to overturn their marriage rules.

00:07:25.653 --> 00:07:29.574
But even as more and more women 
fought and won their cases

00:07:29.574 --> 00:07:33.035
the airlines doubled down on 
the sexy stewardess stereotype.

00:07:33.161 --> 00:07:35.496
“Remember what it was like
before Southwest Airlines?”

00:07:35.788 --> 00:07:37.832
“You didn't have hostesses in hot pants.”

00:07:37.999 --> 00:07:41.002
Around this time, the industry had 
economic problems

00:07:41.002 --> 00:07:44.255
so they decided to lean into the strategy 
that had worked.

00:07:44.505 --> 00:07:46.716
They debuted new ads and uniforms

00:07:46.716 --> 00:07:49.677
that contradicted what the stewardesses 
were fighting for.

00:07:49.886 --> 00:07:53.514
In one campaign called “The Airstrip” 
by Braniff Airlines

00:07:53.514 --> 00:07:55.850
a uniform was designed 
for flight attendants

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:58.269
to shed their clothes
piece by piece

00:07:58.269 --> 00:07:59.854
during in-flight service.

00:07:59.854 --> 00:08:03.483
The airlines were trying to make money 
off of implying

00:08:03.483 --> 00:08:06.402
that we were readily available 
in all sorts of ways.

00:08:06.402 --> 00:08:08.279
And that was just not acceptable.

00:08:08.279 --> 00:08:10.406
But with the Women's Liberation Movement

00:08:10.406 --> 00:08:12.992
there was so much pressure 
and so much encouragement

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:15.369
among the women I flew with to say:

00:08:15.369 --> 00:08:17.288
This is not right, it's not fair.

00:08:17.288 --> 00:08:18.915
We've got to do something.

00:08:18.915 --> 00:08:20.541
And they did.

00:08:20.541 --> 00:08:22.543
Stewardesses started some of the first

00:08:22.543 --> 00:08:25.796
independent, women-led unions 
in US history.

00:08:25.796 --> 00:08:29.133
They also formed groups like 
Stewardesses for Women's Rights

00:08:29.133 --> 00:08:31.093
and tackled age restrictions

00:08:31.093 --> 00:08:34.555
marriage policies, uniforms, 
and weight limits.

00:08:34.805 --> 00:08:36.474
Though much of the mainstream movement

00:08:36.474 --> 00:08:39.268
focused on white women at the time, 
black stewardesses

00:08:39.268 --> 00:08:42.605
were fighting racist appearance standards 
in the industry, too.

00:08:42.647 --> 00:08:44.232
Like one United stewardess

00:08:44.232 --> 00:08:46.776
who was fired for wearing her hair 
in an Afro

00:08:46.776 --> 00:08:51.155
successfully sued and forced the airline
to apply its regulation equally

00:08:51.155 --> 00:08:52.657
without regard to race.

00:08:52.698 --> 00:08:55.952
Many other policies would take 
decades to overturn

00:08:55.952 --> 00:09:00.081
including regulations that grounded attendants 
when they became pregnant

00:09:00.081 --> 00:09:04.919
or weight restrictions, which in one case 
took a 17 year long legal battle

00:09:04.919 --> 00:09:07.964
against American Airlines to finally undo.

00:09:08.839 --> 00:09:12.218
As restrictions changed, so did 
the makeup of the industry.

00:09:12.426 --> 00:09:14.887
Older, married, and 
black stewardesses

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:16.597
increasingly joined the profession.

00:09:16.597 --> 00:09:19.308
The legal fights had altered the airline industry...

00:09:19.392 --> 00:09:21.852
and taken together, they would also alter

00:09:21.852 --> 00:09:24.522
the future of women's labor in the US.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:28.317
The rights that these women won 
have become case law

00:09:28.317 --> 00:09:30.611
about sex discrimination in employment.

00:09:30.611 --> 00:09:34.448
The stewardess cases have been used in 
gender discrimination cases

00:09:34.448 --> 00:09:36.826
in some LGBTQ cases...

00:09:37.034 --> 00:09:41.789
But that all built on a basis of 
stewardess cases, their efforts.

00:09:41.789 --> 00:09:44.542
Which, you know, were back in the 
1960s and 70s

00:09:44.542 --> 00:09:46.210
still have in effect today.

00:09:46.210 --> 00:09:48.838
Those of us that lived in earlier decades

00:09:48.838 --> 00:09:52.633
we were seeking the best possible reality for us.

00:09:52.633 --> 00:09:54.969
I don't think we could ever go back 
to the way it was.

00:09:55.303 --> 00:09:57.013
The history of what happened, frankly

00:09:57.013 --> 00:09:58.598
it was never, ever talked about.

00:09:58.598 --> 00:10:01.601
But it was a beginning.

00:10:02.393 --> 00:10:03.728
Thanks so much for watching.

00:10:03.728 --> 00:10:05.354
My name is Halley,
I’m the producer

00:10:05.354 --> 00:10:07.481
of this episode of Missing Chapter.

00:10:07.481 --> 00:10:10.610
So much research and reporting went into this episode

00:10:10.610 --> 00:10:12.612
and one fact that we didn't get to share

00:10:12.612 --> 00:10:14.196
is that when no-marriage policies

00:10:14.196 --> 00:10:16.365
were rampant in the airline industry...

00:10:16.782 --> 00:10:20.411
it’s estimated that up to 30% of stewardesses
were secretly married.

00:10:20.703 --> 00:10:22.663
Just another one of the many ways

00:10:22.663 --> 00:10:25.124
that they challenged sex discrimination at work.

00:10:25.333 --> 00:10:27.668
We pick stories like this for Missing Chapter

00:10:27.668 --> 00:10:30.630
because it's important to talk about 
underreported history.

00:10:31.088 --> 00:10:34.508
That's also why it's so important 
that we keep this work free.

00:10:34.592 --> 00:10:37.386
But advertising alone isn't enough to support it.

00:10:37.637 --> 00:10:40.640
That's because weeks of work 
go into videos here at Vox...

00:10:41.098 --> 00:10:43.559
scripting, interviewing, editing

00:10:43.559 --> 00:10:46.103
animating, you name it, we do it.

00:10:46.604 --> 00:10:49.523
So if you'd like to help keep this work free
for everyone

